TALLY_FINAL_MESSAGE={"verdict":"transient","diagnosis":"Failed at lane admission before any worktree, worker invocation, or gate execution occurred, so no task-content defect is implicated. Evidence: the failure stage is `lane` with verdict `flow-error` and detail `FlowAdmissionDenied` / code `admission-denied` (ordinal 4, empty details), exitCode is null and captureStderr is empty, gateOutputs is empty, workerOutcomeClaim is null, previousWorkerOutcomes and previousDiagnoses are empty, and the diff is unavailable with reason \"task failed before a worktree was prepared\". Because the flow controller refused to admit the lane, the worker never read specs/flashnext/spec.md#r5 and never created handoff/catalog-row.patch or handoff/README.md; the sole acceptance criterion catalog-patch-wellformed was never evaluated, and there is no failing check id or offending path to cite. Nothing in the task is at fault: it is an implementation task with no dependencies, a single conflict domain of \"handoff\" that is also its only authorized write prefix, and no needs-authority envelope was returned. There is likewise no out-of-task cause to name — no missing authority, no gate-contract defect, no upstream source fix, since the denial carries no adjudicable reason payload and no work product exists to attribute it to. This is a scheduler/session-level admission fault in the campaign machinery (for example lane-slot or conflict-domain admission contention on \"handoff\"), which is precisely what the bounded machinery-retry budget covers; a re-admission of the identical lane at revision sha256:3a04f0fc89c3e88303af3a5b38fc8a4bd52f2f81715bfecfc42bdc04ba5c2269 is the correct next action. If admission is denied again with the same code and still-empty details, the recurrence stops being incidental and should be escalated to the operator as a flow-configuration problem rather than continuing to consume retries."}
